.TH EIGENMATH 1
.SH NAME
eigenmath \- symbolic mathematics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eigenmath
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Eigenmath
evaluates symbolic and numerical expressions, including derivatives,
integrals, exact rational arithmetic, complex numbers, matrices and tensors.
The named files are evaluated in order in a shared context.
A file named
.B -
denotes standard input.
With no arguments, standard input is read;
when both input and output are the console, a question-mark prompt is shown
and each line is evaluated separately.
.PP
Newlines separate expressions. Multiplication can be implicit:
.B "2 x"
means twice
.BR x .
Assignment uses
.B =
and equality testing uses
.BR == .
.B check(e)
stops evaluation if
.I e
is false.
.B run("file")
evaluates another script.
.B exit
or console end-of-file ends the session.
.PP
The assignment
.B tty=1
selects linear text output;
.B tty=0
restores two-dimensional formulas.
Results are written to standard output and diagnostics to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
echo 'd(sin(x),x)' | eigenmath
echo 'integral(x^2,x)' | eigenmath
echo 'det(((a,b),(c,d)))' | eigenmath
eigenmath /sys/src/cmd/eigenmath/examples/oscillator
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/eigenmath
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoc (1),
.IR bc (1),
.IR dc (1)
.br
Eigenmath user guide,
.B https://georgeweigt.github.io/
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Evaluation and I/O errors produce a failing exit status.
A failed script prevents subsequent command-line files from running.
Interactive evaluation errors return to the prompt but retain a failing
session exit status.
.SH BUGS
Scripts and redirected input are limited to 1,000,000 bytes.
Interactive expressions occupy one line.
As in the upstream terminal program,
.B draw
produces no graph.
